refix` — if the flag is \"--output=foo\", it correctly extracts \"foo\", and if it's just \"--output\" without a value, it returns false. Same validation applies to \"--field-separator=\" where single characters are accepted but multi-character separators trigger an error. The implementation looks solid.\n\nI'm also checking the `suffixMultiplier` function's use of bit shifts up to 80. Since Go handles arbitrary precision for untyped constants, `1 << 80` converts cleanly to float64 — it's exactly 2^80, which only needs one significant bit and fits perfectly within float64's 53-bit precision. All powers of 2 up to the maximum exponent are exactly representable, so the multiplication logic should work without precision loss.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14560,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14560},"output_tokens":44,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2bfc84cb-175d-4d01-bbfc-25dae947a6b5","uuid":"2898d308-58c6-44e4-97ab-0fbc8a1031e9"}
